btrfs: complete a checksum-free data read where it finished

Every data read on btrfs is handed to the end-io workqueue before its
completion runs, so that checksums can be verified and a bad sector
repaired from another copy. A read from an inode with no checksums — every
file on a nodatacow volume — has neither to do, and the worker wakeup it
still pays is most of the cost of a cached 4 KiB read: fio at queue depth 1
read at 39k a second on btrfs and 464k on ext4 on the same kernel, with the
device completing inside the submit in both cases.

So a clean data read with no checksums to check completes on the CPU that
found it, as ext4's do. Reads with checksums, reads that failed, and repair
bios keep the workqueue; metadata reads keep it too, for now, since their
completion validates the tree block.

--- a/fs/btrfs/bio.c	2026-09-02 20:55:39
+++ b/fs/btrfs/bio.c	2026-09-02 20:55:39
@@ -355,6 +355,19 @@
 		btrfs_log_dev_io_error(bio, dev);
 
 	if (bio_op(bio) == REQ_OP_READ) {
+		/*
+		 * The workqueue exists to verify checksums and to repair, and
+		 * a data read that returned cleanly from an inode with no
+		 * checksums has neither to do. Completing it here saves the
+		 * worker wakeup, which was most of the cost of a cached 4 KiB
+		 * read. Repair bios keep their path: they are checked by the
+		 * repair code, not by their inode.
+		 */
+		if (!bio->bi_status && is_data_bbio(bbio) && !bbio->csum &&
+		    bio->bi_pool != &btrfs_repair_bioset) {
+			btrfs_bio_end_io(bbio, BLK_STS_OK);
+			return;
+		}
 		INIT_WORK(&bbio->end_io_work, btrfs_end_bio_work);
 		queue_work(btrfs_end_io_wq(fs_info, bio), &bbio->end_io_work);
 	} else {
